		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Majida Gibson]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Sat, 06 Jul 2013 18:39:55 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=432#comment-15137</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[Rabia was a beautiful soul. I met her before she moved to the Abode, and then knew her there. Such strength of character. Such ability to see the good in everyone. To never let difficulties get in her way. She is shining now in whichever heavenly realms she inhabits, and shines to and among us always.
 Majida]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Rabia was a beautiful soul. I met her before she moved to the Abode, and then knew her there. Such strength of character. Such ability to see the good in everyone. To never let difficulties get in her way. She is shining now in whichever heavenly realms she inhabits, and shines to and among us always.<br />
 Majida</p>
]]></content:encoded>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>By: Joel Miller</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=432&#038;cpage=1#comment-15063</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Joel Miller]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Sat, 29 Jun 2013 14:14:01 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=432#comment-15063</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[I got to really know Rabia when she joined to Programs Office.  I had lived in the Abode community and worked in Programs for maybe a year and a half before she joined us. So I knew Rabia and Wali of course, but not super well.  I also did the Sunday Brunches with Mirabai opposite Rabia and Wali.  This was in the Ismail and Hadi days... and just after.  

Rabia joined Programs and was a wonderful and wise counselor to me in the office.  She had a very intuitive and heart-felt sense of people and was so welcoming to all of our guests at the Abode; but she also had a practical streak which I appreciated in terms of planning and running events.  Rabia was so bright and creative and very good and enormously helpful with publicity types of things and she and I produced the catalogue of events one year in what was a wonderful and gentle collaboration.  

Rabia was mischieviously humorus without being malicious and was a good balance to my own jaded skepticism about most things.  I so appreciated our talks about life and about people and about spirit which she was engaged with so dynamically and creatively.   I knew her as a colleague mostly, a wise counselor, a fabulous cook but most importantly for me as
a good friend.  I was deeply honored to have worked and collaborated with Rabia and shocked and saddened by her illness and passing.  I miss her and think of her so often when I think of the Abode and of the Friends.

My Love and Memories of our Gentle and
Creative, and Warm Rabia.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>I got to really know Rabia when she joined to Programs Office.  I had lived in the Abode community and worked in Programs for maybe a year and a half before she joined us. So I knew Rabia and Wali of course, but not super well.  I also did the Sunday Brunches with Mirabai opposite Rabia and Wali.  This was in the Ismail and Hadi days&#8230; and just after.  </p>
<p>Rabia joined Programs and was a wonderful and wise counselor to me in the office.  She had a very intuitive and heart-felt sense of people and was so welcoming to all of our guests at the Abode; but she also had a practical streak which I appreciated in terms of planning and running events.  Rabia was so bright and creative and very good and enormously helpful with publicity types of things and she and I produced the catalogue of events one year in what was a wonderful and gentle collaboration.  </p>
<p>Rabia was mischieviously humorus without being malicious and was a good balance to my own jaded skepticism about most things.  I so appreciated our talks about life and about people and about spirit which she was engaged with so dynamically and creatively.   I knew her as a colleague mostly, a wise counselor, a fabulous cook but most importantly for me as<br />
a good friend.  I was deeply honored to have worked and collaborated with Rabia and shocked and saddened by her illness and passing.  I miss her and think of her so often when I think of the Abode and of the Friends.</p>
<p>My Love and Memories of our Gentle and<br />
Creative, and Warm Rabia.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>By: Shirin Rosenberg</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=432&#038;cpage=1#comment-15061</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Shirin Rosenberg]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Sat, 29 Jun 2013 10:37:03 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=432#comment-15061</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[Beloved Rabia,
Thank you for the loving and giving person you were:
driving me back and forth to work in days when my eyesight was poor; for always making Abode Friday lunch cook-slot fun with our opening circle and your words of &quot;Let&#039;s make something yummy!&quot; for your insight, compassion and common sense observations as Residence Committee chair; for the road trip to Asheville to a Sufi Conference with Zahira Latifa.
But for your friendship and faith in friendship, your astuteness
and kindness, for all that, your lovely being, thank you most of all.  Your light shines on....]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Beloved Rabia,<br />
Thank you for the loving and giving person you were:<br />
driving me back and forth to work in days when my eyesight was poor; for always making Abode Friday lunch cook-slot fun with our opening circle and your words of &#8220;Let&#8217;s make something yummy!&#8221; for your insight, compassion and common sense observations as Residence Committee chair; for the road trip to Asheville to a Sufi Conference with Zahira Latifa.<br />
But for your friendship and faith in friendship, your astuteness<br />
and kindness, for all that, your lovely being, thank you most of all.  Your light shines on&#8230;.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>By: Cynthia Davis</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=432&#038;cpage=1#comment-15058</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Cynthia Davis]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Fri, 28 Jun 2013 22:10:48 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=432#comment-15058</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[Dearest Rabia,

Gone but not forgotten.  I remember her taking me on an excursion to some artist&#039;s estate in upper  New York state.  On that day, I took a photo of a small blue boat with its reflection in the water.  To this day, it reminds me of her gentle, artistic spirit (with some impishness thrown in!) and how she reflected me and I reflected her.  She was an absolute master at dealing with those with special needs (and who of us doesn&#039;t have them?)  Also, hilarious memories of negotiating hard with used car dealers in Pittsfield.  Neither of us bought there.  &quot;Luminous Strands&quot;...a perfect name for her studio/shop and a description of how she connected with so many.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Dearest Rabia,</p>
<p>Gone but not forgotten.  I remember her taking me on an excursion to some artist&#8217;s estate in upper  New York state.  On that day, I took a photo of a small blue boat with its reflection in the water.  To this day, it reminds me of her gentle, artistic spirit (with some impishness thrown in!) and how she reflected me and I reflected her.  She was an absolute master at dealing with those with special needs (and who of us doesn&#8217;t have them?)  Also, hilarious memories of negotiating hard with used car dealers in Pittsfield.  Neither of us bought there.  &#8220;Luminous Strands&#8221;&#8230;a perfect name for her studio/shop and a description of how she connected with so many.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>By: aziza scott</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=432&#038;cpage=1#comment-15054</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[aziza scott]]></dc:creator>
